---
// ===========================================================================
// paths.astro — how a path gets a type, and why it took a registry.
//
// The page exists because the answer is counter-intuitive: the types do not
// travel with the form. Written as the problem first, since the design only
// reads as reasonable once the constraint that forced it is on the page.
// ===========================================================================
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Code from "../components/Code.astro";
import TypeAndInstance from "../components/TypeAndInstance.astro";

const registration = `
// src/waypoint-forms.ts — one file, one declaration, one time.
const orderAdapter = zodFormResolver(orderSchema);

declare module "@maroonedog/waypoint" {
  interface WaypointForms {
    form: typeof orderAdapter;
  }
}
`;

const usage = `
// Any component, at any depth. It imports nothing from the file above.
const postcode = useField("form:billing.postcode");  // string | undefined
const typo     = useField("form:billing.postcod");   // compile error
const sku      = useField("form:items[0].sku");      // a place, checked as its rule
const computed = useField(\`form:items[\${i}].sku\`);   // a computed index is fine
const spliced  = useField(\`form:items[\${s}].sku\`);   // compile error: s is a string
`;

const rows = `
// A row hands down its own address, and the address carries a type:
// \`row.path\` is \`form:shipments[\${number}]\`, not \`string\`.
{shipments.rows.map((shipment) => (
  <Lines key={shipment.key} at={shipment.path} />
))}

function Lines({ at }: { at: \`form:shipments[\${number}]\` }) {
  const lines = useRows(\`\${at}.lines\`);          // checked
  return lines.rows.map((line) => (
    <Text key={line.key} at={\`\${line.path}.sku\`} />   // checked, three levels down
  ));
}
`;

const several = `
declare module "@maroonedog/waypoint" {
  interface WaypointForms {
    order: typeof orderAdapter;
    profile: typeof profileAdapter;
  }
}

useField("order:items[0].sku");
useField("profile:items[0].sku");  // compile error: profile has no items
useField("items[0].sku");          // compile error: two forms, so say which
`;

const dynamic = `
// A schema built from a response, or generated by a benchmark harness.
declare module "@maroonedog/waypoint" {
  interface WaypointForms {
    form: FormAdapter<unknown, string>;
  }
}
`;

const viewTypes = [
  [
    "FormPath",
    "Every registered PLACE, containers included.",
    "A widget that takes whatever it is handed.",
  ],
  [
    "FormPathTo<string>",
    "Only the paths whose value is a string.",
    "A text input. Hand it a number field and it does not compile.",
  ],
  [
    "FormPathOver<'postcode' | 'city'>",
    "Only the prefixes that have all of those underneath.",
    "An address section placed at both billing and shipping.",
  ],
];
---

<Base
  title="Typed paths — waypoint"
  description="One React context serves every form, so the context cannot be generic and a path union cannot travel through it. The types are registered once by the application instead, and a component ten levels down is checked without being handed anything."
>
  <h1>Typed paths</h1>

  <p class="lead">
    <strong>A misspelt field path is a compile error
    instead of an input that renders blank and is never validated</strong> —
    inside arrays, and with a computed index. A typo in an optional-chained
    error lookup is worse than blank: it reads as <em>no error here</em>.
  </p>

  <h2>One declaration, once</h2>

  <Code code={registration} label="file: src/waypoint-forms.ts" />
  <Code code={usage} label="any component, any depth" lang="tsx" />

  <p class="caption">
    <strong class="text-on-surface">A path carries its form.</strong>{" "}
    <code>form</code> is the key declared above, the colon ends it, and the
    value type is read in <em>that</em> form. Register one and the prefix is
    optional; skip the declaration entirely and the hooks are not unchecked —
    they are refused, and the error quotes the declaration you are missing. A
    dependency that registered a form of its own is the exception: registration
    belongs to the compilation, so you inherit that one's paths instead.
  </p>

  <h2>Why a registry and not a prop</h2>
  <p>
    Because the types cannot travel with the instance. One React context object
    serves every form in an application, so the context cannot be generic, and a
    path union cannot pass through it.
  </p>

  <TypeAndInstance />

  <h2>Interpolated paths stay checked</h2>

  <Code code={rows} label="three levels inside a list" lang="tsx" />

  <h2>Several forms are several keys</h2>

  <Code code={several} label="two forms, two keys" />

  <p class="caption">
    With two registered, the unprefixed spellings leave the union. An
    application that grows a second form is told at every call site rather than
    at one. Every path-taking surface gets this for free, including the ones
    with no room for a key beside the path: a{" "}
    <code>&lt;Field path&gt;</code>, a <code>FormPathTo&lt;string&gt;</code>{" "}
    prop, and a row's own <code>row.path</code>.
  </p>

  <p class="caption">
    The key names a form in the <em>registry</em>; the instance still comes
    from the nearest provider, and nothing relates the two. So the provider
    carries its key — name the form at <code>createForm({"{ key: \"order\" }"})</code>{" "}
    and the provider takes it from there — and a path whose head names a
    different form throws, saying which two are being confused.
  </p>

  <p class="caption">
    Where the prefix ends is <em>lexical</em>: a head qualifies a path only
    when it stands before the first <code>.</code> or <code>[</code>. A{" "}
    <code>Record&lt;string, T&gt;</code> key may contain a colon, so{" "}
    <code>byId.a:b.amount</code> stays an ordinary path — the first{" "}
    <code>.</code> comes first. It is a lexical rule rather than "is this head
    registered?" because the run time has to apply the same rule and has no
    registry to consult, only the one key its provider carries.
  </p>

  <h2>Path types for the view layer</h2>

  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead class="text-left">
        <tr>
          <th class="pr-4">Type</th>
          <th class="pr-4">What it is</th>
          <th>Where it goes</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          /* The first row takes no rule: the stylesheet already draws the
             boundary line under the header, and a faint rule immediately
             below it reads as one thick smudge rather than as two ranks. */
          viewTypes.map(([name, what, where], row) => (
            <tr class:list={[row > 0 && "hairline"]}>
              <td class="pr-4 font-mono">{name}</td>
              <td class="pr-4">{what}</td>
              <td>{where}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="caption">
    All three are computed from the registered schema rather than listed, so a
    third address arriving in it needs no edit in the section that draws one.
  </p>

  <h2>What the union costs, and where it stops</h2>
  <p>
    <code>npm run bench:types</code> compiles one throwaway program per shape
    under <code>--extendedDiagnostics</code> and prints the deltas. Like the
    timing lane, it is <strong>printed and never
    gated</strong>: <code>typescript</code> sits at a caret range, so a patch
    bump rewrites every figure without anybody touching a type.
  </p>

  <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr class="hairline">
          <td class="w-64 pr-4 font-medium">Building the union</td>
          <td>
            <span class="measured font-mono">77–137</span> instantiations per
            leaf, once, in whatever file declares the adapter. It does not grow
            with the number of components.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="hairline">
          <td class="pr-4 font-medium">One accepted call</td>
          <td>
            <span class="measured font-mono">129.0</span> instantiations at
            width 300, and <span class="measured font-mono">275.0</span> at
            depth 7. A thousand components addressing one form is not where this
            goes wrong.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="hairline">
          <td class="pr-4 font-medium">The depth budget</td>
          <td>
            <strong>Seven path segments are addressable
            and eight are not.</strong> <code>PathDepthBudget</code> is 6, in{" "}
            <code>src/contract/path-depth.types.ts</code>. A form nested
            past seven segments does not compile slowly — its fields stop being
            addressable at all.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="hairline">
          <td class="pr-4 font-medium">One refused call</td>
          <td>
            <span class="measured font-mono">475,034</span> instantiations,
            because the compiler elaborates the whole union into the message.
            That is a fact about a mistyped path in an editor, not about correct
            code.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="hairline">
          <td class="pr-4 font-medium">The escape hatch</td>
          <td>
            <code>OpaqueObject</code>. A member whose interface extends one of
            its arms ends the path there and keeps its own type and its own
            members, so the container stays nameable and nothing below it is.
            On a 729-leaf shape that took the union from{" "}
            <span class="measured font-mono">99,592</span> instantiations to{" "}
            <span class="measured font-mono">35,508</span> for two of three
            subtrees — exactly the paths given up, and no discount on the rest.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="caption">
    Every figure above is a delta over a floor the document also prints. No
    shape in the sweep produced <code>TS2589</code>, a self-referential root
    included, which is what the budget exists for.{" "}
    <a
      class="link"
      href="https://github.com/maroonedog/waypoint/blob/master/docs/measurements-types.md"
      >The measurement, with what it cannot see →</a
    >
  </p>

  <h2>A schema nobody wrote down</h2>

  <Code code={dynamic} label="a schema with no static shape" />

  <p class="caption">
    <code>ConcretePath&lt;string&gt;</code> is <code>string</code>, so that
    form is back to unchecked paths — by its own declaration, in one place a
    reader can find.
  </p>

  <h2>At run time none of this exists</h2>
  <p>
    Whether a path is real is the <em>store's</em> question, and every caller —
    every hook, every <code>&lt;Field&gt;</code> and every non-React consumer —
    funnels through <code>form.field()</code>.
  </p>

  <!-- Quoted machine output, so it is drawn the way Code.astro draws a code
       block: the sunk ground behind a boundary line, no shadow, the one
       radius. `.verdict` because what the box contains is a judgement the
       library made about a path, which is the colour's whole job. -->
  <div class="mt-6 rounded-[3px] border border-outline bg-surface-low p-5">
    <p class="verdict font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed break-words">
      [waypoint] "order:billing.postcod" is not a field this form has, so it
      will draw nothing and validate nothing. Did you mean: order:billing.postcode,
      order:billing, order:billing.city?
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="caption">
    It <strong class="text-on-surface">warns, once per path; it does not
    throw</strong>. A mis-addressed field is inert, but it cannot let bad data
    through: the pass judges the whole root, so the verdict and the submit gate
    stay correct. The resemblance is searched for with the prefix off, because
    a form's own vocabulary has none in it, then put back on afterwards. Every
    path in the line can be pasted straight back into the source.
  </p>
</Base>
